[h1]Flavorium Universalis — Disease Resistance Hotfix[/h1]

[i]Compatible with EU5 1.3.* [/i]

[hr][/hr]

Vanilla EU5 gives Old World locations only [b]30% starting disease resistance[/b] for smallpox, measles, influenza, and typhus. In observation, disease resistance grows naturally to ~80% by 1490 as pops survive outbreaks and build herd immunity — but that 30% starting floor means early-game outbreaks are far more lethal than the future simulation supports, since these populations had centuries of prior exposure before 1444.

This submod raises the starting resistance for all four diseases across Europe, Asia, and Africa to [b]0.65 (+0.35)[/b], giving Old World locations the baseline immunity they should already have. The Americas and Oceania are untouched — those populations genuinely had zero prior exposure and should remain vulnerable when contact is made.

[hr][/hr]

[h2]What changes[/h2]

[list]
[*] [b]Smallpox, Measles, Influenza[/b] — Old World starting resistance: [b]0.30 → 0.65[/b]
[*] [b]Typhus[/b] — endemic Old World region starting resistance: [b]0.30 → 0.65[/b]
[*] [b]Americas / Oceania[/b] — unchanged (0.0 vanilla)
[/list]

[h2]What it doesn't change[/h2]

[list]
[*] Disease spawn rates, R0 spread values, or mortality script values — only the starting resistance floor
[*] Bubonic plague, great pestilence, or malaria (those have separate vanilla values already accounting for prior exposure)
[*] Anything in the base Flavorium mod
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[i]This is a targeted hotfix while the 1.3 beta is open. Feedback on whether 0.65 feels right is welcome on the discussion page.[/i]
